# Work Discipline

## 1. Full Impact Analysis Before Any Change

Before modifying code, **always** enumerate the complete scope:

- **All affected files**: List every file that needs changes, not just the obvious one
- **All variants/cases**: If the change involves models, types, or modes, create a full matrix (e.g., 5 models × 2 modules = 10 combinations) and verify each cell is handled
- **All downstream consumers**: Check what references, tests, or documentation depend on the changed code

**Never start editing until the full scope is written out.** Partial awareness leads to partial fixes.

## 2. No Piecemeal Fixes

When a change requires touching multiple related things (e.g., adding a model to params, switch logic, and tests), **do them all in one pass**. Do not:

- Fix one thing, upload, then discover the next missing piece
- Wait for the user to point out what was obviously missed

## 3. Changed Files Tracking

When reporting changed files (dev notes, commit messages, PR descriptions):

- **Extract from actual code changes**, not from memory
- Cross-check every component that was touched: code, expect, api, parameters, test
- If you changed `foo/api.imljson`, you almost certainly also changed `foo/expect.imljson` — verify and include both

## 4. Root Cause Over Quick Revert

When something doesn't work:

- **Understand why** before reverting or working around it
- If `switch()` fails with `emptyarray`, ask: "Is `emptyarray` even needed here?" before falling back to `if()`
- Quick reverts mask the real fix and create inconsistency

## 5. Verify Before Submitting

After making changes, **re-read the final state** of the modified file(s) before uploading or committing:

- Does the logic handle every case in the matrix from step 1?
- Are there any leftover inconsistencies from partial edits?
- Does the test cover every case that was added?

## 6. Never Fabricate or Assume

- Do not invent features, directives, APIs, or syntax that you have not verified in documentation or source code
- If uncertain whether something exists (e.g., `temp2`, `switch()` with `emptyarray`), **look it up first** — never try it and see
- "I think it works" is not verification. Read the source.

### Self-Check Before Writing Any Spec Property

Before setting or omitting any property (`type`, `required`, `default`, `spec`, etc.) in expect, parameters, or interface specs, ask yourself:

1. **"Have I seen this exact usage in a reference or existing code?"** — If no, STOP. Look it up.
2. **"Am I writing this because I verified it, or because it feels right?"** — If "feels right", STOP. Look it up.
3. **If not found in references or existing code** — ask the user. Never fill in the gap with a guess.

The same applies to any IMLJSON directive, runtime feature, IML function, or API behavior. **If you cannot point to the source where you verified it, you have not verified it.**

## 7. Learn From the Current Mistake Immediately

When the user points out a missed case (e.g., "editParams has no gptImage15"):

- Before fixing only that one thing, ask: **"Is the same class of error present elsewhere?"**
- Example: if gptImage15 is missing from editParams, immediately check if gptImageMini is missing from generateParams too
- Fix ALL instances of the same error class in one pass, not one at a time

## 8. AC Scope Discipline

- **Never silently add changes outside the AC scope.** If you believe something outside the AC is worth adding, **explicitly highlight it to the user** with the reasoning, and let them decide.
- Format: "**[AC 범위 밖]** {what you want to add} — {why}. 넣을까요?"
- If the user doesn't approve, don't add it.

## 9. Use Prior State as Reference

When converting code from one pattern to another (e.g., hardcoded options → dynamic RPC):

- **Read the original code first** to extract the complete list of what was there
- Do not rely on memory or assumptions about what the original covered
- The original is the source of truth for completeness

## 10. Context Degradation Management

As conversation grows longer, attention and accuracy degrade. Counteract this actively:

- **Never rely on memory for lists** — always re-read files or re-run commands to get current state. What you "remember" from 20 messages ago is unreliable.
- **Re-read before answering** — when asked about what was changed, what's left, or what files were touched, read the actual files/diff instead of reciting from memory.
- **When making a checklist or report** (dev notes, changed files, AC coverage), generate it from code/tools, not from conversation recall.
- **If you catch yourself saying "I already did X"** — verify it. Open the file and confirm. Memory-based confidence is the #1 cause of missed items in long conversations.

### Proactive Reference Re-Read

References read at the start fade from active attention. To prevent hallucination and rule violations, **re-read the relevant reference immediately before each action** — not from memory:

- **Before writing or modifying `api.imljson`** — re-read the relevant references for the work at hand:
  - `communication-reference.md` — HTTP block structure (`url`, `method`, `qs`, `headers`, `body`, `response`, `type`, AWS signing, etc.)
  - `builtin-iml-functions.md` — verify every IML function used in `{{...}}` expressions actually exists
  - `component-patterns-reference.md` — `base.imljson` inheritance, `baseUrl`, common error/header patterns
  - `runtime-reference.md` — runtime directives (`temp`, `condition`, `pagination`, `iterate`, `valid`, `repeat`, `wrapper`, etc.) and middleware execution chain
  - `examples.md` — real app structure and IMLJSON pattern examples (authorize/token/refresh, module api, etc.)
- **Before writing or modifying `functions/*/test.js`** — re-read `custom-functions-reference.md` § "test.js (Required)" for assert conventions (`deepStrictEqual`/`strictEqual`) and required coverage (happy path, edge cases, type variations).
- **Before writing or running component tests** — re-read `component-test-guide.md` for communications mock structure, `transformOutput()`, and assert patterns.
- **Before calling `getJiraIssue`** — re-read `make-app-code-review.mdc` Step 1 for the mandatory `fields` list (`comment`, `customfield_10483`, `customfield_10283`, etc.).
- **Before writing or modifying `interface.imljson`, `expect.imljson`, or `parameters.imljson`** — re-read `parameters-reference.md` for valid types and spec field properties. Re-read `app-ux-best-practices.md` for label, hint, and field conventions. Check existing files of the same type in the same app for patterns. If a type or property is not documented and not used in existing code, do NOT use it — ask the user.
- **Any file, any property, any directive** — if it is not documented in references and not used in existing code, **do NOT use it**. Ask the user instead. This applies universally, not just to the items listed above.
- **Cost of re-reading is always lower than the cost of hallucination** — a Read tool call costs seconds; a fabricated function like `set()` costs a production incident.
